  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "## 13.0.0\n\n### Breaking changes\n- Two generated visuals moved out of the document into the render layer, so they no longer appear as content. An alert's kind header (\"Note\", \"Warning\") is drawn by the alert block instead of being a paragraph inside it, and the notes are nested documents the view shows below the flow above a separator rule, each in a container that draws its number. Two things break for a 12.2.3 consumer. The alert `Section` loses its first child, so a positional selector or code indexing the children of `Section.alertBlock` shifts by one. And the kind text leaves the document, so `ContentRange.GetText()`, select-all/copy and text search no longer see \"Note\" or \"Warning\"; the caret can no longer be placed in the header either. Style the header through `Section.alertBlock`, which reaches it through `FontFamily`, `FontSize` and `Foreground` - all inherited by the body as well; there is no separate selector for it. The notes are no blocks of the document, so no style selector reaches them: a note's paragraphs style like every paragraph.\n- Footnotes are no longer lowered to a superscript hyperlink per citation and loose paragraphs per definition (see Features). Styles or code that targeted that lowering find `RichFootnoteReference` anchors and `Footnote` documents in `FlowDocument.Footnotes` instead; a definition's text is no longer part of the document's text.\n- `MarkdownTaskListItem` now styles as `ListItem`, so `<Style Selector=\"Markdown MarkdownTaskListItem\">` matches nothing - with no compile error and no warning, the styling simply stops. Switch it to `ListItem.taskListItem`. The same change makes the theme's own `ListItem.taskListItem` and `.checked` rules apply for the first time, so an app that never styled task items sees a visual change on upgrade.\n- The dead alert-header theme rules and resources were removed: `BlockUIContainer.alertBlockHeader`, its five `ContentControl.{kind}` icon rules, the five `MarkdownAlertBlockHeader{Kind}ContentTemplate` templates, the `MarkdownAlertBlockHeaderMargin` resource, the five `Section.alertBlock Paragraph.{kind}` rules and the `MarkdownAlertBlock{Kind}ParagraphForeground` brushes. Nothing produced the elements they selected, so none of them ever applied; a consumer overriding one of those resource keys had no effect and still has none.\n- `MarkdownSerializer` declares `CanRead`, and gains the synchronous `Deserialize(Stream, CancellationToken)`, `Serialize(DocumentSnapshot, Stream, CancellationToken)` and `Deserialize(string)` the other text-format serializers already had. See the `Avalonia.Controls.Documents` changelog for the interface change.\n- `MarkdownSerializer.SerializeAsync` and `DeserializeAsync` are removed; `Serialize` and `Deserialize` are the whole contract. Parsing and writing markdown is processor-bound and assembles its output in memory, so the async pair was a thread-pool hop that held a pool thread for the duration of synchronous work. Replace `await serializer.SerializeAsync(snapshot, stream, ct)` with `serializer.Serialize(snapshot, stream, ct)`, or `await Task.Run(() => serializer.Serialize(snapshot, stream, ct), ct)` to keep it off a UI thread.\n- `CodeHighlighter` can be derived from. Its constructor was `internal` and its abstract `Highlight` method `internal` too, so only the two bundled adapters could subclass it and an application with its own lexer had nowhere to plug in. The constructor is `protected`, `Highlight` is `protected abstract`, and `BuildHighlightDocument` is `protected internal static`. `ColorCodeHighlighter` and `TextMateHighlighter` now override `Highlight` as `protected`, exactly as a third-party highlighter does.\n- `Markdown.CodeHighlighter` and `Markdown.ImageLoader` put the two extension points on the control a consumer actually places. Both are inheriting attached properties, so they reach every code block and image in the control's document, and `MarkdownCodeBlock.Highlighter` or `MarkdownImage.ImageLoader` set on one element still wins. Attaching a highlighter previously required a style setter targeting an element type the consumer had to know the control built internally, and was invisible from C#.\n- `Markdown.Copy()` becomes `Task<bool> Markdown.CopyAsync()`. An `async void` method cannot be awaited, so a caller could not tell when the clipboard write finished, and a failure from `IClipboard.SetTextAsync` - common on Linux and in headless hosts - was raised on the synchronization context and took the process down instead of reaching the caller. The result is `false` when there was nothing to copy, a `CopyingToClipboard` handler took over, or the control has no top level.\n- `MarkdownSerializer` configures through its constructor: `new MarkdownSerializer(options, codeHighlighter)` replaces `new MarkdownSerializer { Options = options, CodeHighlighter = h }`, and both properties are read-only. Every other serializer takes its options that way and documents itself as thread-safe; this one could not, because the Markdig pipeline was built by an unsynchronized lazy assignment two concurrent reads raced on. The pipeline is built in the constructor now and the type is thread-safe for concurrent operations.\n- `MarkdownSerializer` observes its `CancellationToken`. It was accepted and ignored: the whole parse plus snapshot render ran uninterruptibly and `MarkdownWriter.Write` took no token at all. Reading and writing now check per block, which matters most for the streaming path the package exists to serve. The Markdig parse ahead of rendering still runs to completion once entered.\n- `MarkdownSerializer.CanDeserialize` returns false for a null or unreadable stream instead of an unconditional true. A format-sniffing loop stopped at markdown whatever the stream's state; markdown still accepts any readable stream, so it has to be tried last.\n\n### Features\n- `MarkdownSerializer` now writes markdown: `Serialize` no longer throws, and a `Serialize(DocumentSnapshot)` string convenience was added. Documents loaded from markdown (or authored with the markdown-specific elements) round-trip to an equivalent document; output spellings (bullet marker, emphasis and fence characters, ordered delimiter, hard-break form) are configurable via the new `MarkdownSerializerOptions` on the `Options` property. Grid-table writing and YAML front matter are not covered.\n- New typed markdown elements make fidelity data survive editing sessions and enable authoring through the FlowDocument API: `MarkdownHeading` (`Level`), `MarkdownAlertBlock` (`AlertKind`), `MarkdownHtmlBlock` / `MarkdownHtmlInline` (`RawHtml`), plus `MarkdownImage.AltText`.\n- Footnotes load onto the document's own footnote model instead of a markdown-only one: the first citation of a label becomes the note's `RichFootnoteReference` anchor, numbered in citation order, and the definition becomes a `Footnote` in `FlowDocument.Footnotes` carrying its label as the new `Footnote.Label`, so the notes render, paginate, print and export like every other document's notes and the editor's footnote commands work on them. Later citations of the same label, and citations inside a note, become `RichFootnoteCitation` elements pointing at the same note: they show its number without storing it, so adding or deleting a note renumbers every citation of it, no digit reaches the document's text, and they still write back as `[^label]`. Clicking one scrolls to its note, as clicking the anchor does; a note created in the editor is named by its position when written.\n- Unedited HTML blocks write back their original source verbatim; unrecognized inline HTML tags are preserved instead of dropped.\n- Footnote citations navigate: clicking an anchor or a further citation scrolls its note into view. Previously nothing handled a citation's `#label` target, so the click reached the platform launcher with a relative URI and went nowhere. A fragment that names no note - `[Back to top](#top)`, a heading anchor - is left unhandled and keeps bubbling, so an application implementing its own anchors still receives it.\n- `MarkdownCodeBlock.InfoArguments` carries the rest of a fence's info string after the language identifier, so directives such as ` ```js {highlight=3} ` or ` ```text title=foo ` survive a save.\n- `MarkdownNodeKinds.Marked` gives a `==marked==` span a kind of its own, and its element carries the `marked` CSS class the shipped theme styles.\n\n### Fixes\n- The parser no longer loses data it cannot show: link titles, image alt text, footnote definition labels and grouping, the pipe-table header row, and CommonMark angle autolinks (previously dropped entirely) are all captured now; a missing image title is no longer replaced by the URL, and footnote back-links no longer pollute definition text.\n- The typed markdown elements use their base type's style key (`MarkdownHeading` styles as `Paragraph`, `MarkdownAlertBlock` as `Section`, and so on), so theme and consumer selectors such as `Paragraph.h1` or `Section.alertBlock` keep matching; style these constructs via their CSS classes, not their type names. `MarkdownCodeBlock` and `MarkdownImage` keep their own keys as documented. `MarkdownTaskListItem`'s move to the `ListItem` key is a silent break - see Breaking changes.\n- An inline HTML tag left unclosed no longer discards the rest of the document. `Some <b>bold text here.` followed by another paragraph parsed to a document whose entire text was \"Some \", which is the state every streaming flush passes through between a model emitting `<b>` and emitting `</b>`. An unclosed tag now formats to the end of its block.\n- An inline HTML close tag closes only the container its own tag opened. A stray `</b>` is preserved as raw HTML instead of vanishing from the file, and it can no longer end a link or an emphasis it did not open.\n- A footnote definition nothing cites is kept. Opening a notes file holding `[^todo]: check this later` and saving it deleted the line; a file of definitions only opened and saved empty.\n- A footnote label containing `]`, `[` or `\\` is escaped on write. It was written raw, which ended the citation early and turned both the citation and its definition into literal text: one save destroyed the note.\n- A code span whose content is only spaces no longer grows by two characters on every save.\n- A table whose first row is not the header gets an empty header row instead of having its first row of data promoted into one. This affected grid tables, tables that reached the model from another format, and tables whose header row was deleted in the editor.\n- An ordered task list keeps its numbering: `1. [x] done` was written back as `- [x] done`.\n- A `==marked==` span is recognized by its kind, so recolouring the highlight no longer makes the next save write it as plain text with no `==`.\n- An alert kind outside the theme's five (`> [!INFO]`, `> [!DANGER]`) falls back to the note styling instead of reserving a border thickness with no brush to paint it.",
